#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228860 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e885f33-5bef-5c95-8afa-495b6512e398" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/id #uuid "d77c4a1b-863b-57ad-be77-44f2d802f554" :question/math-academy-id "e-21677" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2 - xy^2, \\qquad y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2).$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2-x{y}^{2})+(2-{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4-x{y}^{2}-{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) \\\\  & =2-0.5x{y}^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2-0.5x{y}^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^2 + y_\\text{new} - y - 2 + 0.5xy^2 = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $\\Delta x = 1$ is\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^2 + y_\\text{new} - y - 2 + 0.5xy^2.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.\n$$\n\n Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5\\cdot 2\\,({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-2+0.5\\cdot 1\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{g(y_\\text{new})}{g'(y_\\text{new})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\ng'(y_\\text{new}) = 2(y_\\text{new}) + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1}\n= (y_\\text{new})_n\n- \\dfrac{((y_\\text{new})_n)^2 + (y_\\text{new})_n - 2.5}\n       {2(y_\\text{new})_n + 1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1) = 1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈1.166\\,667 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.158\\,333 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.158\\,312\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{1.16},$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228861 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05af3055-c035-5e11-8946-8791c21a18ac" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/id #uuid "9148676f-5899-539c-9b95-4a0d8d225056" :question/math-academy-id "e-21679" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2y^2 - \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x = 1,$ the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - 1 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - \\dfrac12 (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 - \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2{y}^{2}-\\sqrt{x}+2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - y - y^2 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x} + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x_\\text{new}} = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $\\Delta x = 1$ is\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - y - y^2 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x} + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x_\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1-{1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1}+\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e885f33-5bef-5c95-8afa-495b6512e398" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "749a0cfe-3551-5baf-8285-a83252b85f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "749a0cfe-3551-5baf-8285-a83252b85f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333916} :answer-field/id #uuid "527790c6-44ec-5f59-950d-f51dddb0ae56" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3a732f7-f589-5b15-910f-f8c16bf26587" :question/math-academy-id "q-333426" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-{y}^{3},\\,y(0.5)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x-{y}^{3})+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}))\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5x-0.25{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5x-0.25{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.25{y}^{3}-y-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.25{y}^{3}-y-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5x.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.25(1)^{3}-1-0.5\\cdot 1-0.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.25(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1.5}{0.75(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0.5)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈1.142\\,857 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.134\\,757 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.134\\,728\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.13,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05af3055-c035-5e11-8946-8791c21a18ac" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e9f8d27-70b2-58d0-a80d-31c7a51ac774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333918} {:answer/id #uuid "b56644e6-5146-553a-b338-da8749918b44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333919} {:answer/id #uuid "19a942f7-5602-5c71-8097-607bf71894b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333920} {:answer/id #uuid "168cad73-980c-56ed-897a-9495f1e87542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333921} {:answer/id #uuid "c315ff79-1bb7-5512-84b6-1201333c9fb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b56644e6-5146-553a-b338-da8749918b44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333919} :answer-field/id #uuid "451eb28e-07a4-5856-bd47-213a04e47257" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "372050b3-acc3-51df-a046-2619f3775d3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333430" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y},\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=0.5,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **general form** of $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ in terms of the new values ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and the current values $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+4({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05af3055-c035-5e11-8946-8791c21a18ac" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c9d0f16-1bb1-5e63-a51a-60b19fec88e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333925} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3b51c7-1d21-5d34-9079-c675a33ee41c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{3}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333926} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd09e61-06b2-55c1-9546-0c251a8c2d98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333927} {:answer/id #uuid "583bf341-f0b6-593b-a788-b787413b6372" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-\\frac{3}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333928} {:answer/id #uuid "39638e4b-1bf1-5765-ac95-32b69415b33a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dd09e61-06b2-55c1-9546-0c251a8c2d98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333927} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e51697f-0a7d-5bd5-9c3e-704b73bf4896" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ccc8cee-f0e6-56e9-91a7-6163287662cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-334067" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3{x}^{3}\\sqrt{y},\\,y(0)=4\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=0.5,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **general form** of $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ in terms of the new values ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and the current values $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{x}^{3}\\sqrt{y}+3({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{3}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{3}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{{{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{y}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{y}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05af3055-c035-5e11-8946-8791c21a18ac" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2d30ce9-1686-5b8b-8453-2d4c3ab9bbb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-y-{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333932} {:answer/id #uuid "4efc7768-7cc0-5a7a-ab61-f21b4b9efd90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333933} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6ef9f3-0021-5ac9-9bbf-5009d36df410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-2{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333934} {:answer/id #uuid "f717f9ca-a1fd-5a10-97cd-39da2ec18533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333935} {:answer/id #uuid "9068941f-1c93-52bd-a001-ae95a6fd0cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4efc7768-7cc0-5a7a-ab61-f21b4b9efd90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333933} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c4e8d6-8cd5-51b8-80e8-3ad8370434f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70d609a4-8f19-5100-ad16-3405717a65a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334069" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{x}^{2}y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=0.5,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **general form** of $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ in terms of the new values ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and the current values $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4{x}^{2}y+4({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}^{2}y+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-{x}^{2}y}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-{x}^{2}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05af3055-c035-5e11-8946-8791c21a18ac" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5069878e-3917-5973-95ad-04c24ac24542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333939} {:answer/id #uuid "56245661-beaf-5590-89a8-cdc843dfede2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-y-\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333940} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf0426e-fb07-554c-ab9d-2d04c4f79160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{5}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333941} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0a1805-cd92-538a-9e81-eed8f881129e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333942} {:answer/id #uuid "6a616447-b9b8-5764-bfe0-847e9151ffd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a616447-b9b8-5764-bfe0-847e9151ffd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333943} :answer-field/id #uuid "b309d559-fd64-559c-a448-6259ea655225" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce80911a-49f2-51c7-9291-5f4522b313cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334070" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5{x}^{3}y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=0.5,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **general form** of $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ in terms of the new values ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and the current values $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(5{x}^{3}y+5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y+\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y+\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{{{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05af3055-c035-5e11-8946-8791c21a18ac" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea3a259-9de2-5c30-acf0-969554ff4fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-8({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333946} {:answer/id #uuid "ac071946-c43b-5f00-a590-1c5af5682f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333947} {:answer/id #uuid "babc3af7-b8e9-54c1-8dce-0c7280c00c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-8({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333948} {:answer/id #uuid "e31e9de5-e78f-5477-87f1-ce14ebb8ddf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-8({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333949} {:answer/id #uuid "743a50e9-4c15-5856-88d4-fa27e7a977a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "babc3af7-b8e9-54c1-8dce-0c7280c00c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-8({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333948} :answer-field/id #uuid "c422d4a1-b9e2-50e8-8992-162a9a762d4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c3a7627-c377-5c3a-851c-bcadd27ed02f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334075" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}-2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\n Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-2(2)^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1-2(1)^{2}(1)^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-8({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e885f33-5bef-5c95-8afa-495b6512e398" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52386a81-83af-5451-910b-16dae66bbc34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52386a81-83af-5451-910b-16dae66bbc34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333953} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7422642-e58a-58ca-badf-cda7c998faa3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60f08bf3-3155-5b9a-98b6-e147ed21f307" :question/math-academy-id "q-334197" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+4{y}^{2},\\,y(0.5)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ starting from $x=0.5,$ we use a step size\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-x+4{y}^{2})+(-{x}_{\\text{new}}+4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}))\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =-0.25x-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =y-0.25x-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}^{2}+y-0.25x-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}^{2}+y-0.25x-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in this step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+0+0-0.25(0.5)-0.25(1) \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.375.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y(0.5)=0$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})}{{g}^{′}(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=2{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}^{\\,2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-0.375}{2({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1}=y(0.5)=0,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈-0.375 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈-0.29464 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈-0.29058 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{5} & ≈-0.29057\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈-0.29,$\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e885f33-5bef-5c95-8afa-495b6512e398" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68284d95-ee18-5ff7-a2e6-ff8199f9c578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333956} {:answer/id #uuid "4f033d62-cc65-57a8-98e7-48568bedd9db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68284d95-ee18-5ff7-a2e6-ff8199f9c578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333956} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c72a69c-15b6-5f76-a3d5-43f62e6c6f54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0037370c-41e1-56de-bcdc-f8c10918ac15" :question/math-academy-id "q-334426" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(0.4).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(0.4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0.4,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0.4-0=0.4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x+{y}^{2})+({x}_{\\text{new}}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}))\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =0.2x+0.2{y}^{2}+0.2{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.2x+0.2{y}^{2}+0.2{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+y+0.2x+0.2{y}^{2}+0.2{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $5$ to eliminate the decimals, we obtain\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}+5y+x+{y}^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.4$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}+5y+x+{y}^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+0.4=0.4.\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}+5\\cdot 0+0+{0}^{2}+0.4 \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=0$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=2({y}_{\\text{new}})-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}-5({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}+0.4}{2({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-5}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=0,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =0.08 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈0.081\\,322 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈0.081\\,323\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.4)≈0.08,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e885f33-5bef-5c95-8afa-495b6512e398" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2985367d-cb43-5c51-8eed-abbb0db812d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333960} {:answer/id #uuid "efe9b5ca-8337-5cba-8f91-55c4ce871ce5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2985367d-cb43-5c51-8eed-abbb0db812d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333960} :answer-field/id #uuid "60086b88-7e2f-50c6-95fa-939ab8812401" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51bc4d54-4dd4-521c-9e72-90bab859c94d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334469" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-{y}^{3},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((3x-{y}^{3})+(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.5x-0.5{y}^{3}+1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+1.5x-0.5{y}^{3}+1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5{y}^{3}-y-1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-1.5x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5{y}^{3}-y-1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-1.5x.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\n Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5(1)^{3}-1-1.5\\cdot 1-1.5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5-1-1.5 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-2}{1.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.2 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.179\\,747 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.179\\,509\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.18,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e885f33-5bef-5c95-8afa-495b6512e398" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60ca3656-a347-589f-9ec7-cfbd117a6d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60ca3656-a347-589f-9ec7-cfbd117a6d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333964} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae3c4ab2-0bd3-5aed-b6fd-0ca5533de55b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6c43cc2-f4b5-59ac-b529-c1877515e05f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334473" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+{y}^{3},\\,y(1)=-1.5\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x+{y}^{3})+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =x+0.5{y}^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+x+0.5{y}^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+y+x+0.5{y}^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+y+x+0.5{y}^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\n Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+(-1.5)+1+0.5(-1.5)^{3}+2 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.5+1-1.6875+2 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.1875.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=-1.5$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}-({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-0.1875}{1.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1)=-1.5,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =-1.5 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈-1.342\\,105 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈-1.310\\,301 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈-1.309\\,019\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-1.31,$ rounded to two decimal places."}]}
